<section class="container home-about" id="on-the-bench" aria-labelledby="bench-title">
  <div>
    <p class="eyebrow">On the bench now</p>
    <h2 id="bench-title">Speaker measurement</h2>
    <p>I’m working on a low-cost way to measure, compare, and keep track of loudspeaker drivers without turning the process into a pile of screenshots and spreadsheets.</p>
    <p>The measurement hardware is still in development. DriverBench is the software side of that work and is available now as an early preview.</p>
    <a class="text-link" href="/driverbench-preview/">Take a look at DriverBench →</a>
  </div>
  <div class="home-notes">
    <h3>Why I’m building it</h3>
    <p>I wanted a repeatable way to test drivers, keep the measurements, and compare what changed. The current project grew out of solving that problem on my own bench.</p>
    <p>No big product pitch yet. I’m building it, testing it, and figuring out where it is actually useful.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="container home-about" aria-labelledby="built-title">
  <div>
    <p class="eyebrow">Something I built</p>
    <h2 id="built-title">DevGuard 24</h2>
    <p>DevGuard started because I wanted something between my bench supply and whatever questionable prototype I was powering that day.</p>
    <p>It’s a small inline board for 3–24 V DC projects with reverse-polarity, overcurrent, and transient protection.</p>
    <a class="text-link" href="/products/devguard-24/">See DevGuard 24 →</a>
  </div>
  <div class="home-notes">
    <img class="tool-photo" src="/images/power-checker-hero.jpg" width="2048" height="1151" alt="DevGuard 24 inline DC protection module" loading="lazy">
    <p>Not everything here needs to become a giant product line. If something earns a permanent spot on my bench, it has a decent chance of ending up here.</p>
  </div>
</section>

